EARLY='Baseline breast and nodal imaging, with staging tests appropriate to the diagnosis. Review response during preoperative treatment and before surgery. After local treatment, arrange breast surveillance; routine CT, PET or tumour-marker testing is not the usual screening plan for someone without symptoms.'
MET='Record baseline disease sites. Response imaging is generally reviewed every 2–4 months under international guidance, adapted to symptoms, disease pace and treatment. The team selects the scan; this is not an automatic CT chest/abdomen/pelvis order for every patient. Tumour markers may support assessment in selected cases, but do not alone decide progression or a treatment change.'
BONE='If the cancer is also hormone-receptor positive, endocrine treatment may form part of the wider plan. Bone-density testing is linked to aromatase inhibitors, ovarian suppression and fracture risk; it is not automatically required because a HER2 medicine is prescribed. Agree baseline DEXA where indicated and individual follow-up.'
HEART='Check heart pumping function before starting HER2 treatment. Repeat echo or MUGA every 12 weeks: around cycle 5, then cycle 9 if 21-day cycles stay on time. Earlier checks are needed for symptoms or abnormal findings. Count elapsed time from the first HER2 dose; changing phase or route does not restart the clock.'
RTNEO='Systemic treatment comes before surgery. Lumpectomy or mastectomy is chosen with the surgical team. Radiotherapy, if indicated, follows surgery and recovery; it may overlap postoperative HER2 treatment when both teams agree. It is not automatically concurrent with the chemotherapy phase.'
RTADV='Surgery or radiotherapy may be used for particular symptoms or disease sites. There is no routine surgery-then-radiation sequence for this advanced-cancer regimen. The oncology and radiation teams agree any treatment pause or overlap.'
POST='After surgery, the pathology helps select the next HER2 medicine. The plan usually aims to complete about one year of HER2 treatment from the first dose. Residual invasive disease may change the treatment. Blood tests and supportive medicines must follow the selected postoperative regimen.'
